WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.070
(lively electronic music)

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:03.610
- Back in the old days,

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:05.580
this part of the world used to be

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.300
the economic superpower of the planet.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.670
There were dynasties that
discovered everything

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:13.070
from the compass, to printing,

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:15.520
to making paper, to gunpowder.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.800
They would lead huge
expeditions around the globe.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.390
This was China, the Middle Kingdom,

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:22.923
and right next door, India.

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:27.740
This was the center of the
global economic universe.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:31.269
But around the 1800s,
that started changing.

00:00:31.269 --> 00:00:34.500
(photos click)

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.000
Over here, in Europe
and the United States,

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.223
there was a major revolution
that started taking place.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:44.480
Science, then machines, then weapons,

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:47.580
trains, cars, flight, bombs,

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.590
factories, cities, and
with their new power,

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:53.130
these countries started to expand

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:56.430
to take over large swathes of the map.

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:58.370
China remained a great civilization,

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:00.940
but as the west started showing up,

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:04.470
knocking on China's door
with their big guns,

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:06.590
China couldn't really
match their firepower,

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:09.230
and they had to concede land to Britain,

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:11.010
which is how we got Hong Kong.

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.790
The industrial revolution
didn't really happen in China

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:15.620
the same way it did in the west,

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:17.350
and by the middle of the 1800s,

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:19.680
China began a massive decline

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.750
in its economic standing in the world.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:24.340
A century later, that
situation got way worse

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:26.880
as China was invaded
by its neighbor, Japan.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:30.530
China's economy was no longer
the prosperous Middle Kingdom,

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:32.760
but rather a shadow of itself,

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:35.860
a poor country that was
being occupied by outsiders.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:38.380
Once World War II was over,

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:42.540
the west devised a new global
economic and financial system.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.930
It was modeled after the economic system

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:47.140
that was working so well in the west,

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.290
capitalism, free enterprise,

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:51.210
still with some government oversight

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:53.660
and high taxes for the
rich and corporations,

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:55.190
but it was still capitalism,

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:57.710
and after the war, this
capitalism would go on

00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:00.600
to become a miracle for many countries,

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:03.680
rebuilding Germany, France, Japan.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:05.020
For many in the west,

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:08.510
post-World War II capitalism
was like a magic elixir

00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:10.390
that was fixing everything.

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:11.610
It was working so well

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:13.730
that more and more
people started to believe

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:16.270
that we needed to get
government out of capitalism,

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:18.880
and let the market just do its thing.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:21.170
One of the major advocates for this idea

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:24.370
of unfettered capitalism was this guy.

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:27.380
- There has never in history
been a more effective machine

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:28.580
for eliminating poverty

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:30.800
than the free enterprise
system and the free market.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:34.140
- A Chicago-based economist
named Milton Friedman.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.620
- If they fail, they bear the cost.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:39.617
If they succeed, they get the benefit.

00:02:39.617 --> 00:02:42.680
- The idea at the core of this financial

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:47.210
and economic system was that
companies would get investment

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:49.700
from people who owned
shares in that company,

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:51.100
we call this a corporation,

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:53.870
and that the sole purpose
of this corporation

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:57.130
was to make money for these
owners, or these shareholders.

00:02:57.130 --> 00:02:58.740
In Milton Friedman's own words,

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:00.887
quote, "The social responsibility

00:03:00.887 --> 00:03:03.680
"of a business is to increase profits."

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.170
That is the rule of a business.

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:08.280
Increasing short term
gains for shareholders

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.320
would benefit all of society,

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:11.740
according to this model.

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:13.100
And if you look at the data,

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:15.050
in the United States and the west,

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:17.113
Milton Friedman sort of had a point.

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:19.343
(horn honks)

00:03:19.343 --> 00:03:22.160
And it led to huge prosperity in the west,

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:25.640
as the U.S. economy exploded.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:27.430
Wait a minute, but what about China?

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:29.160
Aren't we talking about
China and its economy?

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:31.090
You know, the Middle Kingdom, China.

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:33.530
So, while the U.S. and
western economies are thriving

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:36.040
because of this corporate
free market capitalism

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:37.660
that was taking over the world,

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:41.460
China was descending further
and further into decline.

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:43.710
Instead of joining the capitalism party

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:44.800
that the west was spreading

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:47.150
throughout the world, China resisted,

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:51.150
turning to a one-party-rule
communist system,

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:53.850
headed by this guy, Mao Zedong,

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:55.780
who destroyed any chance

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:58.480
of China's economy ever
joining the prosperity

00:03:58.480 --> 00:03:59.880
happening around the world.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:03.930
By the 1970s, China was a
place of widespread poverty.

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:07.840
The once prosperous kingdom
had reached rock bottom,

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:09.600
but that was all about to change.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.690
(film projector clicks)

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:14.000
So it's the 1970s,

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.490
and this is China's new
leader, Deng Xiaoping.

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:20.730
He came into power hoping to turn around

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:24.470
this 150 years of humiliation in China,

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:27.100
the decline of China as a superpower.

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:28.380
Elsewhere in the region,

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:30.860
China's neighbors had economies

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:33.570
that joined the global economic party

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:35.570
that was happening in capitalism,

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:37.170
and they were thriving.

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:39.770
They saw immense economic miracles,

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:41.910
and pulled millions out of poverty.

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:44.860
Deng specifically went
on a visit to Singapore

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:46.450
early on in his term

00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:50.540
where ethnic Chinese had
adopted the free market,

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:52.180
and they were thriving.

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:53.680
He felt inspired.

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:55.380
The Maoist economic doctrine

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:57.700
that had failed was over,

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:01.940
and it was time to see if China
could rebuild its economy,

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:04.970
experimenting with some
version of capitalism.

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:06.380
But he had to do this carefully

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:08.650
so as not to disrupt the one-party rule

00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:09.990
that China had created,

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:11.910
so he decided to just focus

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:14.060
on one little village,

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:16.620
a sleepy village in southern China

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:19.530
of just around 30,000 people,
right across the border

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:21.680
from the British-controlled Hong Kong,

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:23.390
which, at the time, was thriving

00:05:23.390 --> 00:05:26.020
because of its hardcore
capitalistic economy.

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:29.110
Deng Xiaoping designated this little town

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:32.340
as, quote, a special economic zone

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:34.220
where foreign companies could set up

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:38.400
and invest in a very
capitalistic free market.

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:40.700
Hong Kong would sort of act as a bridge

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:44.190
between western businesses
and this little gateway

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:45.660
into communist China.

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:47.050
It was still socialism,

00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:51.580
but with some Chinese
capitalistic characteristics.

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:54.070
What happens next is arguably

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:57.200
the most mind-blowing economic miracle

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:00.045
ever to have taken place on planet Earth.

00:06:00.045 --> 00:06:04.126
(lively electronic music)

00:06:04.126 --> 00:06:05.630
This little, sleepy village

00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:08.820
that was a special economic
zone in China exploded

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:10.950
from a little fishing village

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:12.727
with little more than
a few thousand people

00:06:12.727 --> 00:06:16.230
and some rice paddies to a massive city

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:18.650
of over 10 million people.

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:21.070
The average income went from $1 a day

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:24.110
to over $30,000 a year.

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:26.180
This starts happening all over China,

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:28.210
and meanwhile, Milton Friedman's back

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:29.478
in the U.S., being like,

00:06:29.478 --> 00:06:31.570
(lively orchestral music)

00:06:31.570 --> 00:06:33.050
yep, told ya.

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:36.180
Soon, there were a bunch of
cities designated in China

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:38.480
for special economic activity,

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:41.210
or, basically, capitalism
in a socialist country,

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:44.570
and more and more foreign
companies flocked to China

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:46.070
for its cheap labor.

00:06:46.070 --> 00:06:48.100
It became the world's factory.

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:51.300
China joined this trend
of other Asian economies,

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:52.580
and eventually passed them up

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:55.470
to become the second
largest economy on earth.

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:56.850
You can see this in a graph.

00:06:56.850 --> 00:07:00.160
This year, Asia will actually
pass the rest of the world

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:03.470
in accounting for more
than 50% of global GDP.

00:07:03.470 --> 00:07:06.290
It is a massive economic transformation,

00:07:06.290 --> 00:07:09.620
and it explains why China
has become so powerful.

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:10.860
Okay, but wait,

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:14.550
this is a very wonderful
story about capitalism

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:18.730
and its ability to lift millions
of people out of poverty,

00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:22.190
and to industrialize a
country seemingly overnight,

00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:24.160
but there's another side to this coin

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:26.489
that we need to talk about.

00:07:26.489 --> 00:07:27.322
(paper rustles)

00:07:27.322 --> 00:07:28.810
I have two graphs I wanna show you.

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:31.030
(paper rustles)

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:32.333
They look like this.

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:35.440
Okay, let's look at this one first.

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:37.780
It's called the Elephant Graph.

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:38.798
Do you sorta see why?

00:07:38.798 --> 00:07:39.780
(elephant trumpets)

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:41.460
Let's deconstruct this graph,

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:44.300
and try to understand what this says

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:47.590
about the past 30 years
of the rise of China.

00:07:47.590 --> 00:07:52.590
On this axis is everyone on
earth ranked by their income,

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:55.400
grouped into percentiles,
so these people down here

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.870
make an income that is
the 10th percentile,

00:07:57.870 --> 00:08:00.060
the lowest 10% in the world,

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:02.940
and then the 20th percentile,
and so on, and so on,

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:05.220
up to these people up here who
are the richest in the world.

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:08.370
They have an income that puts
them in the 90th percentile.

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:10.800
This axis here shows how much growth

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:13.070
and income these people have had

00:08:13.070 --> 00:08:15.270
over the past 35 years,

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:18.090
basically during the heyday
of shareholder capitalism

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:19.100
and the rise of China.

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:20.970
So, you'll see how this
plays out in just a second.

00:08:20.970 --> 00:08:23.670
Let's start plotting
how much income growth

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:27.110
each of these groups saw
over the past 35 years.

00:08:27.110 --> 00:08:29.580
The bottom 10% saw their incomes grow

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:32.440
by around 75%, not bad.

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:35.120
These are people who live
in the poorest countries,

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:37.010
who make the lowest income in the world.

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:42.010
The 20-30% saw huge
growth in their income,

00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:45.030
125% and more over the past 35 years.

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:47.360
This is like the new middle class in China

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:48.710
and other Asian countries

00:08:48.710 --> 00:08:52.430
who saw huge income growth
due to globalization.

00:08:52.430 --> 00:08:54.740
They did really well under this system.

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:57.160
But then, you start to get to the people

00:08:57.160 --> 00:08:59.620
in the 50th, and 60th, and 70th,

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:01.500
and 80th percentile.

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:03.610
This is basically people who live

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:05.670
in the middle class in the west.

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:08.810
Their incomes have grown much slower,

00:09:08.810 --> 00:09:11.860
sometimes under 50% over
the course of 35 years,

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:15.040
which is little over 1% a year,

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:16.720
not very much income growth,

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:18.480
and some of these people in the west

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:20.800
have seen their incomes shrink,

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:22.680
like the blue collar workers in Detroit

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:25.430
and the Rust Belt whose
jobs disappeared to China.

00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:28.930
So, so far, you can sort of
see why this makes sense.

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:30.970
The people in China who benefited

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:35.890
from this mass industrialization
saw huge benefits,

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:37.650
and the people here in the United States

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:42.210
and in the west who saw
jobs go overseas to China

00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:44.810
saw a way slower growth in their income.

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:47.200
But watch what happens next,

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:48.790
when we get to the groups of people

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:51.170
who are at the 99 percentile,

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:54.060
basically the richest 1% in the world,

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:56.610
and for this, I actually
have to stretch out the graph

00:09:56.610 --> 00:09:58.530
to get us a little bit more granular.

00:09:58.530 --> 00:09:59.363
These are the people

00:09:59.363 --> 00:10:01.460
who have the highest incomes in the world.

00:10:01.460 --> 00:10:04.440
Let's see how their income has grown

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:06.893
over the past 35 years.

00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:11.580
You can see that their income has exploded

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:13.030
under this system.

00:10:13.030 --> 00:10:15.310
They're seeing tripling of their income

00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:16.880
in the past 35 years.

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:19.420
Now, a reminder that
this is 35 years of data

00:10:19.420 --> 00:10:21.060
from every economy on earth.

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:22.740
This shows everyone on the planet.

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:25.570
So this gives us a view
of who are the winners

00:10:25.570 --> 00:10:28.760
and the losers of this
shareholder capitalism

00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:31.600
that has globalized to
Asia and around the world.

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:34.690
The top 1% of income earners

00:10:34.690 --> 00:10:37.650
saw the biggest benefits from this system.

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:40.220
They are the ones who
disproportionately benefit

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:42.040
from shareholder capitalism

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:43.913
and the growth of globalization.

00:10:43.913 --> 00:10:45.030
(soft electric piano music)

00:10:45.030 --> 00:10:46.270
Okay, so that's my first graph.

00:10:46.270 --> 00:10:48.020
It's the Elephant Graph.

00:10:48.020 --> 00:10:52.130
It is a very useful way
to see one of the results

00:10:52.130 --> 00:10:55.650
of this version of capitalism
and the growth of China.

00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:58.623
The second graph looks like this.

00:11:00.190 --> 00:11:04.170
This shows the amount of
CO2 emissions by country.

00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:06.390
You've got Europe and the United States,

00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:08.220
and then, up here, you've got China,

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:10.870
the world's largest emitter of CO2.

00:11:10.870 --> 00:11:12.340
Like the Elephant Graph,

00:11:12.340 --> 00:11:14.800
this graph combines loads of data

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:18.960
to show us that shareholder
capitalism focuses so much

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:21.350
on short term profits for shareholders

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:23.520
that it leaves out any consideration

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:27.497
for how this economic activity
affects the planet we live on

00:11:27.497 --> 00:11:29.250
and our ability to live on it.

00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:31.310
I know you've heard this message a bunch,

00:11:31.310 --> 00:11:32.370
and if you're like me,

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:35.170
it is hard to not become sort of numb

00:11:35.170 --> 00:11:38.210
to this impending crisis

00:11:38.210 --> 00:11:39.680
that, we don't really
know what it looks like,

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:41.410
and it feels like there's
nothing we can do,

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:43.460
but we have to do
everything really quickly.

00:11:43.460 --> 00:11:45.880
But let me just reiterate in my own words

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:48.060
that it is now a certain fact

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:50.800
that our global climate is changing

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:54.270
in such a way that it
will very likely lead

00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:57.050
to immense shifts on our earth,

00:11:57.050 --> 00:11:59.190
and our ability to live on our earth.

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:01.740
It's already happening, it
will continue to happen,

00:12:02.890 --> 00:12:04.170
and all we can do now

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:06.170
is maybe put on the brakes a little bit.

00:12:09.490 --> 00:12:11.080
Okay, let me just wrap this up,

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:12.380
and give you my final thought

00:12:12.380 --> 00:12:14.060
on what this all means.

00:12:14.060 --> 00:12:15.400
I'm not showing you all these graphs

00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:16.740
because I'm just, like, a data nerd

00:12:16.740 --> 00:12:17.990
who loves to look at graphs,

00:12:17.990 --> 00:12:19.770
although I kind of am.

00:12:19.770 --> 00:12:20.603
Sorry.

00:12:20.603 --> 00:12:21.436
I'm showing you these graphs

00:12:21.436 --> 00:12:23.050
because the story of the rise of China

00:12:23.050 --> 00:12:26.580
is often a story of how
capitalism has pulled tens

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:28.230
and hundreds of millions out of poverty,

00:12:28.230 --> 00:12:29.770
and it has, and that's amazing,

00:12:29.770 --> 00:12:32.780
but these graphs give us a view

00:12:32.780 --> 00:12:35.850
of what it has also
done, what this version

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:39.510
of capitalism also
incentivizes and results in.

00:12:39.510 --> 00:12:41.520
If we're going to consider the effects

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:43.520
of this version of capitalism,

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:45.810
we have to look at all of its effects,

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:46.710
not just some of them.

00:12:46.710 --> 00:12:48.890
So, what's the solution?

00:12:48.890 --> 00:12:51.090
Is it socialism?

00:12:51.090 --> 00:12:52.440
No, it's not.

00:12:52.440 --> 00:12:54.960
It is simply a new version of capitalism,

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:56.950
a reframing of how we think

00:12:56.950 --> 00:12:58.920
about business and globalization,

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:02.210
one that old Milton
Friedman would despise,

00:13:02.210 --> 00:13:03.970
and one that more and more businesses

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:06.330
are actually realizing we must adopt.

00:13:06.330 --> 00:13:09.490
The solution isn't to scrap
capitalism altogether,

00:13:09.490 --> 00:13:12.140
but instead to expand it,

00:13:12.140 --> 00:13:14.200
to not just focus on shareholders

00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:15.790
and delivering them short term profits

00:13:15.790 --> 00:13:18.000
as quickly and as efficiently as possible,

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:20.390
but to also focus on other people

00:13:20.390 --> 00:13:22.970
who are affected by the business.

00:13:22.970 --> 00:13:24.950
You can call these stakeholders.

00:13:24.950 --> 00:13:28.310
This is like the customers,
and the suppliers,

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:30.090
and the employees, and the government,

00:13:30.090 --> 00:13:32.680
and the overall economy, and the planet,

00:13:32.680 --> 00:13:34.880
and, of course, shareholders.

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:36.960
This is called stakeholder capitalism,

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:39.010
and it's basically just an acknowledgment

00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:41.350
that businesses that focus only on profits

00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:44.330
for shareholders don't end
up benefiting everyone.

00:13:44.330 --> 00:13:46.260
There are winners and losers.

00:13:46.260 --> 00:13:49.690
It's not good for a huge
swathe of the population,

00:13:49.690 --> 00:13:51.400
nor for the changing climate.

00:13:51.400 --> 00:13:54.650
Oh, and by the way, this isn't
some fringe liberal idea.

00:13:54.650 --> 00:13:56.740
Some of the biggest companies
here in the United States

00:13:56.740 --> 00:13:58.670
like Walmart, Apple, JPMorgan,

00:13:58.670 --> 00:14:02.410
have signed onto adopting
a new model of capitalism.

00:14:02.410 --> 00:14:06.450
This is changing, and in
the next five to 10 years,

00:14:06.450 --> 00:14:09.020
I believe we will see a new version

00:14:09.020 --> 00:14:11.330
of how businesses interact in the economy.

00:14:11.330 --> 00:14:12.320
So you're probably wondering,

00:14:12.320 --> 00:14:15.180
listen, I clicked on a video
about the rise of China,

00:14:15.180 --> 00:14:18.340
and we're ending here with
stakeholder capitalism.

00:14:18.340 --> 00:14:21.620
The rise of China is
a story of capitalism,

00:14:21.620 --> 00:14:23.760
a certain version of capitalism.

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:26.720
It's a story about how
capitalism can morph

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:30.110
and change depending on what
effects we want businesses

00:14:30.110 --> 00:14:31.870
to play in our society.

00:14:31.870 --> 00:14:34.120
If we want to produce
the most amount of wealth

00:14:34.120 --> 00:14:35.650
in the most efficient way,

00:14:35.650 --> 00:14:37.660
then the old shareholder model

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:39.640
actually really works well for that,

00:14:39.640 --> 00:14:42.270
but if you want businesses
to benefit everyone,

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:44.710
including our children,
and our grandchildren,

00:14:44.710 --> 00:14:46.400
and their ability to live on this planet,

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:49.930
then our capitalism needs
to pull in more priorities

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:51.880
than just prophets for shareholders.

00:14:51.880 --> 00:14:54.000
- [Milton] What may be right for one stage

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:55.230
in the development of an economy

00:14:55.230 --> 00:14:57.170
may not be right for another stage.

00:14:57.170 --> 00:14:58.790
- [Reporter] The so-called
greenhouse effect

00:14:58.790 --> 00:15:00.670
is created by carbon dioxide,

00:15:00.670 --> 00:15:02.850
a colorless, odorless gas.

00:15:02.850 --> 00:15:05.960
- So, I got to dig through a
lot of data for this video,

00:15:05.960 --> 00:15:09.030
